course: geography
lesson: 57
title: Fossils
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A fossil is a message from a living thing that died long ago, usually more than 10,000 years ago. It can be a bone or shell turned to stone, a print of a leaf, or even a footprint.
explain: Most fossils form when a dead animal or plant is buried quickly in mud or sand, before it rots or gets eaten. Layer after layer piles on top, the mud slowly turns to rock, and minerals soak into the bones and shells.
explain: Picture Mary Anning, a girl from a poor family in Lyme Regis, hunting along stormy cliffs in the early 1800s. She found the first complete ichthyosaur skeletons, giant sea reptiles, and gentlemen scientists wrote papers about her finds, often without her name.
explain: Because so few living things are buried just right, only a tiny fraction ever become fossils. Still, they tell us that many dinosaurs had feathers and that birds are living dinosaurs.
fact: A fossil is the preserved remains or trace of a living thing from long ago, usually over 10,000 years old.
fact: Most fossils form when a dead plant or animal is quickly buried in mud or sand, which later turns to rock.
fact: Only a tiny fraction of living things ever become fossils.
fact: Trace fossils record what animals did, such as footprints, burrows and even fossil dung.
fact: Ammonites were sea creatures with coiled shells, related to squid, and are very common fossils.
fact: Mary Anning, a working-class woman from Lyme Regis, found the first complete ichthyosaur skeletons in the early 1800s.
fact: Some fossils are used to date rocks, because the species lived only for a short time but spread widely.
fact: Some fossils keep soft parts: insects trapped in amber, and woolly mammoths frozen in Siberian ice.
fact: The first dinosaur to be scientifically named was Megalosaurus, in England in 1824.
fact: Many dinosaurs had feathers, and birds are living dinosaurs.
fact: Long before science, people explained fossils with legends, such as griffins, perhaps from beaked dinosaur skulls found in the deserts of Central Asia, or "snakestones" from ammonites.
exercise: What is a fossil? | The preserved remains or trace of a living thing from long ago | Any old stone with a funny shape that you happen to find in a field | A bone from last year's dinner | A rock shaped by the wind
exercise: Fossils are usually older than about how many years? | 10,000 years | 10 years | 100 years | 1,000 years
exercise: How do most fossils form? | A dead plant or animal is buried quickly in mud or sand that turns to rock | A dead animal lies out in the hot sun for many years until it goes hard as stone | Animals turn to stone when they are frightened | Lava covers living animals and keeps them soft
exercise: How many living things become fossils? | Only a tiny fraction | About half of them, roughly | Almost all of them, sooner or later | Every single one that ever lived
exercise: What is a trace fossil? | A record of what an animal did, like a footprint or burrow | A drawing of a dinosaur made by a scientist from its bones | A bone that has been broken | A copy of a fossil made of plaster
exercise: Which of these is a trace fossil? | Fossil dung | A dinosaur skull | An ammonite shell | A mammoth tusk
exercise: What were ammonites? | Sea creatures with coiled shells, related to squid | Land snails the size of houses that lived in forests | Early flying reptiles | Small fish with spiky fins
exercise: What did Mary Anning find in the early 1800s? | The first complete ichthyosaur skeletons | The first gold ever found in Australia | The first diamond in India | The first frozen mammoth in Siberia
exercise: Where did Mary Anning live and hunt for fossils? | Lyme Regis, on the English coast | Paris, in a big museum by the river | New York, on a farm | Cairo, near the pyramids in the desert
exercise: Why are some fossils useful for dating rocks? | The species lived only briefly but spread widely | They glow brighter and brighter as they get older | They show the year they died on their shells | They are always found at the same depth
exercise: Where can soft parts like insect wings be kept? | In amber | In sandstone at the beach | In boiling lava | In river water
exercise: Where have whole woolly mammoths been found frozen? | In Siberian ice | In the Sahara sand | In Amazon mud | In Hawaiian lava
exercise: What was the first dinosaur to be scientifically named? | Megalosaurus | Tyrannosaurus | Diplodocus, the long neck | Triceratops, the horned one
exercise: When and where was Megalosaurus named? | In England in 1824 | In China in 1924 | In America in 1724 | In Egypt in 1524
exercise: What do fossils tell us about birds? | Birds are living dinosaurs | Birds came before all fish | Birds evolved from bats | Birds have nothing to do with dinosaurs
exercise: What did people long ago call ammonites? | "Snakestones" | "Dragon teeth" | "Moon rocks" | "Fairy eggs"
test: Ben reckons every dead animal turns into a fossil eventually. Right? | No, only a tiny fraction ever become fossils | Yes, every one does after about 100 years | Yes, but only the big ones | No, only animals that died in caves or under the sea
test: A row of three-toed prints in old rock shows an animal walking. What kind of fossil is it? | A trace fossil | A body fossil of bones | An amber fossil | A frozen fossil
test: Why does a fish that dies and is buried in mud at once have a better chance of becoming a fossil than one that dies on a beach? | Quick burial protects it from rotting and scavengers | Fish on beaches slowly turn into sand grains in the sun | Mud is warmer than sand | Beaches have no oxygen
test: A film shows a scientist cutting a fossil open and finding fresh dinosaur blood. What's wrong? | Fossils are usually stone; fresh blood does not last millions of years | Dinosaurs had no blood | Fossils are hollow inside | Dinosaur blood was solid gold
test: Two cliffs far apart both contain the same short-lived ammonite species. What can geologists say? | The rocks are probably about the same age | The rocks are completely different ages, millions of years apart | The cliffs were once one giant ammonite | Ammonites lived in both cliffs today
test: ugh my brother says "dinosaurs were all scaly lizards, birds are totally different". Fix it. | Many dinosaurs had feathers, and birds are living dinosaurs | Dinosaurs were all furry mammals | Birds are a kind of lizard, not dinosaur, he's half right | Dinosaurs were all bald
test: People in ancient times found huge bones with beaks in the desert. What legend may this have led to? | Griffins | Mermaids | Unicorns made of glass | Talking trees
test: Why was Mary Anning's work often not credited in her time? | She was a poor working-class woman, and men wrote up her finds | She never found anything at all, she only sold seashells to visitors | She hid all her fossils | She only found fake fossils
test: What would happen to our knowledge of the past if no mud or sand ever buried dead things? | Almost no fossils would form, so we would know far less | We would have even more fossils, lying about on the ground | Fossils would form in the sky instead | Nothing would change
test: A mosquito in a golden stone looks almost alive with wings and legs. Why so perfect? | Amber sealed it and kept its soft parts | It was frozen solid in Siberian ice | It is a living mosquito sleeping inside | It was painted on by a jeweller
test: Which is a fossil? | An ammonite shell turned to stone | A seashell washed up on the beach last week | A plastic dinosaur toy | A chicken bone from lunch
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a fossil? | The preserved remains or trace of an organism of considerable antiquity | Any stone of irregular form discovered in the soil | The skeleton of an animal deceased within the present year | A replica of an organism fashioned in plaster
exam: Identify the principal condition favouring the formation of a fossil. | Rapid burial of the organism in sediment subsequently lithified | Prolonged exposure of the organism to sunlight upon the open ground | Immersion of the organism in molten lava | Consumption of the organism by scavengers
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the use of fossils in the dating of rocks. | Species of brief duration but wide distribution permit rocks to be correlated in age | All fossils bear an inscription of the year of death | The size of a fossil is proportional to its age | Fossils are invariably situated at an identical depth
exam: Ascertain the relationship which is presently accepted between birds and dinosaurs. | Birds are themselves dinosaurs, descended from feathered forms | Birds are descended from bats | Birds preceded all dinosaurs by a billion years | Birds and dinosaurs are wholly unrelated
